<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vitcos</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vitcos is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> archaeological site in the Vilcabamba region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manco-inca">Manco Inca</a> made it a residence after withdrawing from Cusco in 1537, and he was killed there in 1544 by Spanish refugees he had sheltered. Nearby lies the great carved sacred rock of Yurak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rumi">Rumi</a>, also called Chuquipalta. Hiram Bingham identified the site in 1911.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vitcos.jpg" alt="Vitcos" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Malmestar8 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vitcos_-_Rosaspata_(parcial).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
